    "minutes": 9,
    "sections": [
      [
        "The quiet week can decide the analyzer",
        "Count accepted patient results by assay, including the demand in quieter weeks. Controls, calibration, repeats and opened reagent that expires all consume supplies without producing additional reportable patient results. A panel contains several tests, so a specification of 200 tests per hour does not mean 200 complete patient panels. HumaLyzer 4000 is a lower-complexity chemistry comparison when trained staff can prepare and pipette the required work. BK200 automation becomes valuable when handling time is the demonstrated bottleneck and the laboratory can sustain its water, room conditions and repairs. The BK200 page specifies 15–30°C operation and deionized-water consumption below 5 L/hour. Include the actual water-treatment and room-control provision; do not assume maximum published consumption equals the site’s measured bill."
      ],
      [
        "Put capital and results into the same period",
        "For a simple three-year illustration, assume US$18,000 installed capital, $12,000 recurring expense each year and 6,000 accepted results each year. Total cost is $54,000 and total results are 18,000, giving $3.00 per result. Dividing the three-year cost by only one year’s results would incorrectly give $9.00. Now assume lower activity of 3,000 accepted results a year reduces recurring expense only to $10,000 annually, because service, QC and pack wastage do not fall in proportion. Three-year cost becomes $48,000 for 9,000 results, or about $5.33 each. These are hypothetical inputs, not analyzer prices. They show why the quiet-week reagent basket and fixed obligations matter. Use either a consistent multi-year cost model or an annual model with an explicit annual capital allocation. Include all purchased reagent and control packs once; do not add the same expired or repeated-test reagent again as a second expense. Keep cash requirements visible alongside the unit-cost calculation."
      ],
      [
        "Buy the differential the laboratory actually requires",
        "XQ-320, pocH-100i and XP-300 belong to the three-part differential comparison. A five-part requirement calls for a different capability, such as the identified human BK-6310 configuration. Extra differential detail can justify spending when required; it does not establish better uptime or a cheaper accepted result. For occasional CBC demand, pocH-100i’s lower-volume positioning deserves consideration alongside reagent open stability and control costs. For higher demand within the three-part requirement, assess XQ-320. Retain an existing XW-100 only with its actual supply, connectivity and support arrangement confirmed. An online support portal does not prove new-unit availability or portable offline suitability."
      ],
      [
        "Spend bench money where the method needs it",
        "OMAX M82ES-A188V’s dated US$317.99 offer provides a budget microscopy candidate, not a guarantee of diagnostic suitability. Assess prepared-slide images, focus stability, stage movement and illumination for the intended work. Compare CX23 where its exact clinical configuration and optical service justify the difference. High total magnification alone is a poor substitute for useful resolution and a stable working view. For centrifugation, the tube method determines force, rotor geometry and capacity. HORIZON 6 Flex provides six-place horizontal centrifugation up to 2,000 ×g; EBA 200 offers eight-place fixed-angle operation up to 3,461 ×g. These are different arrangements, not a simple ranking by speed. Include the correct holders and adapters, and distinguish EBA 200 from the higher-force 200 S."
      ],
      [
        "A new microtome cannot finish an incomplete histology service",
        "BIOCUT and HM325 are concrete manual microtome alternatives for an established histology pathway. Compare the fitted specimen clamp, blade holder, consumable blades and demonstrated section quality. An accepted existing instrument may deserve repair when those parts remain supportable. If tissue processing, embedding, staining, ventilation, interpretation or quality assurance is the missing step, buying a newer sectioning instrument leaves that gap open. Fund the actual bottleneck or a dependable referral route. The same rule applies across this shortlist: spend for a defined useful capability and the resources that sustain it, rather than an unsupported brand reliability ranking."
      ]
    ],
