        {
          "heading": "The real choice is shared or separate capacity",
          "paragraphs": [
            "An upright attachment can bring biopsy guidance into a mammography room already in use. A dedicated prone system separates that work from the mammography schedule. Neither arrangement is automatically better: the clinical team must first establish which procedures and patient-positioning needs the offered configuration can meet.",
            "For a modest accepted workload, sharing equipment may avoid a second imaging chain and room. For a busier service, the displaced mammography appointments, setup and turnover can make dedicated capacity worthwhile. Count the actual blocked time rather than comparing only the acquisition price."
          ],
          "sourceIds": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Use compatible examples, not family-name assumptions",
          "paragraphs": [
            "Hologic describes its Affirm guidance system as an add-on for compatible Hologic 3D-capable mammography systems, with 2D and 3D guidance. Its Affirm prone product is a dedicated prone 2D/3D system. These are examples of the two architectures, not a finding that any used Hologic unit accepts any Affirm attachment.",
            "Record the host system, software version, guidance device, licences and accessories in one configuration schedule. Have the supplier document compatibility and the clinical team review the intended positioning. Optional access hardware should appear in the offer when the accepted procedure plan depends on it."
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "affirm-up",
            "affirm-prone"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Price the appointments that sharing displaces",
          "paragraphs": [
            "Use a representative monthly schedule. Suppose, hypothetically, 12 biopsy visits each require a 90-minute block in a shared room, including preparation and turnover. That occupies 18 room-hours. It does not mean every hour would otherwise produce a completed mammogram: staff availability, patient demand and the shape of the schedule determine what is displaced.",
            "Compare that realistic loss of usable capacity with the fixed cost of a dedicated service. Include time to configure and restore a shared room. A dedicated machine earns its cost when it resolves a meaningful bottleneck or accepted positioning need, not simply because it leaves the mammography unit free."
          ],
          "sourceIds": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Guidance is not the whole biopsy package",
          "paragraphs": [
            "List the sampling system, approved disposable devices, associated consoles or vacuum equipment, specimen handling and any required specimen imaging separately. Hologic's prone product page identifies compatible sampling products; their presence in a catalogue does not prove they are included in an equipment sale.",
            "Carry the workflow through specimen identification, transport, pathology reporting and patient follow-up. Price the approved supply basket and expected wastage for the actual volume. A guidance system can sit unused if a necessary single-use item or pathology route is unavailable."
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "affirm-prone"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Review the room with the patient and team in mind",
          "paragraphs": [
            "A dedicated table needs a current installation assessment covering access, room layout, services and radiation protection. Review how the team positions and assists patients, reaches controls and moves supporting equipment. An old planning drawing for a different table is not a current minimum-room specification.",
            "A shared room also needs a practical trial. Check storage for the attachment and accessories, setup, transfer and the return to ordinary imaging. Agree acceptance, training and quality responsibilities for the complete offered arrangement. Clinical suitability and safe working practices require the responsible team's assessment, not a purchasing-page inference."
          ],
          "sourceIds": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Account for the consequences of one failure",
          "paragraphs": [
            "Sharing a host system concentrates dependence: a failure can interrupt both mammography and biopsy. Dedicated equipment separates some of that exposure while creating another system to maintain. Neither arrangement supplies a backup pathology service or an available clinical team.",
            "Keep referral in the comparison, especially for low or uncertain volume or procedures outside the accepted local scope. Choose the arrangement that can deliver the required work consistently, with a funded response when equipment or supplies are unavailable. The best purchase may be an attachment, a separate room or dependable access elsewhere."
          ],
          "sourceIds": []
        }
