    "equipmentId": null,
    "title": "Vascular ultrasound: a linear probe is not the whole package",
    "summary": "Compare the examinations, Doppler work and reporting the service needs. A portable and a shared imaging cart can each be good value for different workloads.",
    "categoryId": "imaging",
    "category": "Imaging & radiology",
    "minutes": 3,
    "sections": [
      [
        "Define the vascular work before choosing the platform",
        "A buyer should first separate the planned vascular examination service from a bedside device intended for a narrower task such as access guidance. Ask the vascular lead to define the examinations, patient range, measurements and reporting. A shared word in a product category does not make the equipment packages interchangeable. Match the required work to probes and enabled functions. Keep any additional probe needed for a different depth or examination on its own line. The cheaper offer is not equivalent if it covers only the easiest part of the brief or requires an unpriced software addition to produce the intended report."
      ],
      [
        "Use exact examples to test the offered configuration",
        "GE’s August 2024 LOGIQ E10 image gallery identifies L2-9-D in a carotid PW waveform example. Its current product page also identifies that probe in a lower-extremity venous micro B-Flow example. Those illustrations support specific questions about the offered package; they do not prove every E10 includes the same probes, software or release. For a compact alternative, the current Vivid iq page identifies L4-20t-RS and restricts that probe to Premium and 4D versions. That is a useful reason to check the version, even where the buyer does not need 4D cardiac work. A quotation headed simply “Vivid iq with linear probe” omits a distinction that can affect the purchase."
      ],
      [
        "Make the report part of the demonstration",
        "Have the qualified vascular team evaluate the complete task using its approved acceptance arrangements. Follow the required imaging and measurements through saved studies, labels and the report. Establish which values transfer into the reporting workflow and which still require manual entry. Count the actual steps before accepting a promised productivity advantage. A demonstration on a supplier’s fully equipped unit should not settle a purchase of another configuration. Record the release, enabled options and probe used. If an advanced flow display is attractive, ask whether it changes a requirement in the clinical brief; otherwise compare it as an optional benefit rather than an assumed necessity."
      ],
      [
        "A shared cart and a portable solve different access problems",
        "A shared imaging cart may provide useful capability across departments and spread its ownership cost. It can also be unavailable when the vascular service needs it. A portable may remove that scheduling or location constraint while introducing its own display, battery, cart and transport requirements. Compare access to the complete package, not console weight alone. Consider a service whose existing cart performs the required examinations but spends the planned vascular session in another department. A dedicated accepted package addresses access; buying another high-end option for the shared cart does not. Conversely, when availability is adequate, an additional supported probe or reporting connection may be the better investment."
      ],
      [
        "Keep older systems in the comparison without inventing support",
        "A working legacy platform with accepted probes and a credible repair route can remain useful. A low used price is less persuasive when essential transducers, analysis rights or support are uncertain. Assess console and probe condition separately and compare repair, replacement and temporary cover for the exact equipment. The sources do not establish that a cheaper legacy model equals a newer platform’s clinical performance or has a longer useful life. Choose from demonstrated task completion, access and complete ownership cost. Pay more where it closes a named gap; retain the installed service where it already supplies the result the team needs."
      ]
    ],
