    "id": "equipment-exit-buying-guide",
    "key": "equipment-exit",
    "kind": "research-guide",
    "equipmentId": null,
    "title": "Selling medical equipment: the highest bid can leave less behind",
    "summary": "Compare sale, donation, redeployment and retirement after removal, data handling and transition costs. A simple net-proceeds example shows why the biggest offer may be the weaker deal.",
    "categoryId": "decisions",
    "category": "Procurement & service",
    "minutes": 3,
    "sections": [
      [
        "Compare the four routes before accepting an offer",
        "Sale may recover cash. Redeployment may preserve useful capacity within the organization. Donation may meet a recipient’s real need. Retirement may be the appropriate route when a safe, supported onward use cannot be established. Compare each route against the same responsibilities and transition date; none makes removal or unresolved obligations disappear. For redeployment or donation, establish that the receiving service actually wants the exact configuration and can fund installation, operation and support. Moving an incomplete system does not create a working service. For retirement, plan qualified decommissioning and the applicable disposal route rather than leaving unwanted equipment indefinitely in a corridor or store."
      ],
      [
        "A smaller gross offer can produce a larger return",
        "Consider a hypothetical sale with two offers for the same equipment. Bid A is US$10,000, but the owner must pay US$7,000 for the agreed removal and transaction costs. Net proceeds are US$3,000. Bid B is US$7,500 with US$1,500 of owner-paid costs for the same obligations, leaving US$6,000. The lower bid leaves US$3,000 more. These are illustrative amounts, not equipment valuations. The comparison works only if scope and risk are genuinely comparable. Check dismantling, packing, lifting, transport, storage, commissions, required data work and any remaining lease or contract payments. Show exclusions and unresolved costs explicitly instead of deducting an optimistic single allowance."
      ],
      [
        "Describe what is actually available to transfer",
        "Prepare an accurate equipment identity and condition record: model and serial, installed options, included accessories, known faults, maintenance history and any relevant restrictions. Distinguish what has been tested from what is merely present. Useful documentation helps the recipient judge the package; it does not guarantee a resale premium. Check ownership, outstanding obligations and the rights associated with software and hosted services. Possession of the machine should not be treated as proof that every licence or service account transfers with it. Resolve the permitted handover and recovery route before promising a working software package to a buyer or recipient."
      ],
      [
        "Patient-data deletion needs its own completion evidence",
        "For US covered entities, HHS says policies and procedures must address final disposition of electronic protected health information and removal before media reuse. NIST’s sanitization overview frames the objective as making access to target data infeasible for a defined level of effort. Neither a blank patient list nor a “Delete All” screen proves that this objective has been met. Assign authorized technical and privacy staff to determine the applicable process for the actual device and storage media. Preserve required records through the approved route, protect any necessary recovery configuration and obtain evidence of completion. Do not improvise a wipe command from a general equipment article; requirements and suitable methods depend on the system and jurisdiction."
      ],
      [
        "Finish the transition as well as the transaction",
        "Agree who disconnects and removes the equipment, what access and preparation are required, who bears damage risk, and when custody changes. Define the evidence that closes each step. A buyer collecting from the loading dock has not necessarily accepted responsibility for removing a heavy installed system from its room. Keep clinical continuity in the decision. If the replacement is not yet accepted, an early pickup may create an expensive service gap. Compare holding, temporary cover and staged removal where appropriate. Close the asset, service and data records once the agreed work is complete; a payment receipt alone should not mark the entire exit finished."
      ]
    ],
