    "equipmentId": null,
    "title": "Pain RF generators: the electrode system can outweigh the console price",
    "summary": "Compare the complete approved generator, electrode, cannula and cable package. Reusable supplies, single-use devices and specialist functions create different continuing costs.",
    "categoryId": "surgical",
    "category": "Surgery & anesthesia",
    "minutes": 3,
    "sections": [
      [
        "The clinical brief decides which RF functions matter",
        "A pain-service generator should be specified for the clinician’s intended procedure, approved instruments and supported functions. A general electrosurgical generator or a broad “RF” label is not enough to establish that package. The responsible clinical team must decide the appropriate treatment; the purchasing comparison then determines what is needed to provide it. Abbott’s IonicRF page describes four independently controlled channels and several RF modes. It also states that the generator’s intended use relies on approved compatible electrodes and cannulas. More channels are valuable only when the agreed workflow uses them; they are not evidence that a package gives better pain outcomes."
      ],
      [
        "Separate the ordinary case basket from specialist additions",
        "Price the exact electrodes, cannulas, cables, required return electrode and other supplies for the intended case. State what is reusable, what is single-use and what processing is required. Keep specialist additions separate so that an infrequently used accessory does not distort the comparison of routine work. Simplicity III illustrates the dependency. Abbott describes this single-use electrode as requiring a compatible Abbott RF generator and an Abbott adapter cable. An offer naming the generator and electrode but omitting that connection is incomplete. Do not infer compatibility with an older NeuroTherm model or a third-party cable from brand resemblance. Reusable and single-use routes move costs between purchases and processing. Compare the approved reusable item’s acquisition, inspection, care and replacement with the continuing single-use basket. Do not invent a number of safe reuses or spread the price of a single-use device across several procedures."
      ],
      [
        "Compare the same work over the same period",
        "Take an explicitly hypothetical one-year example: System A costs US$6,000 to acquire and requires US$180 of comparable supplies per case. System B costs US$10,000 and requires US$120 per case. At 100 cases, the respective totals are US$24,000 and US$22,000. The higher purchase price leaves a US$2,000 lower acquisition-plus-supply total. These are invented comparison inputs, not offers for any named model. The example excludes staffing, imaging, processing, service, financing and downtime. Add any differences in those costs before choosing. Its point is to make a recurring supply difference visible; it does not establish that either system is clinically equivalent or cheaper in your setting."
      ],
      [
        "A used console needs a continuing accessory and support route",
        "When considering a used NT2000iX or an installed system, obtain the exact model, software, enabled functions and approved accessory list. Compare it with a complete newer offer, rather than carrying old reseller specifications into a current promise. The apparent saving is uncertain if essential electrodes or service cannot be supplied. Include the clinical and technical teams in acceptance of the delivered combination. Confirm any required imaging, patient monitoring and coexistence arrangements from the applicable instructions, and assign competent training and support. No universal electrode placement, stimulation or lesion settings belong in a purchasing guide. A supported repair or compatible replacement instrument may preserve useful existing capacity. A new platform becomes more attractive when it supplies a required function or a sustainable accessory route that the old service lacks. Judge that improvement against the full conversion, continuing case costs and temporary cover—not touchscreen size or a promise that one RF mode is painless."
      ]
    ],
