    "title": "CUSA and ultrasonic aspirators: the handpiece can change the deal",
    "summary": "A console-only price misses the tools, tips and processing needed for surgery. Compare the exact application package and a sustainable supply of every case item.",
    "categoryId": "surgical",
    "category": "Surgery & anesthesia",
    "minutes": 4,
    "sections": [
      [
        "Choose the handpiece and application before the console",
        "An ultrasonic surgical aspirator belongs to a defined surgical service. It is not an ultrasonic instrument cleaner, and a generic “CUSA” listing does not specify what the surgeon can use. The clinical team must identify the intended application, approved handpiece and tip combination before procurement compares systems. A console that powers on can still omit the required handpiece, footswitch, tubing or processing accessories. Ask for the complete part-number list and have the qualified clinical and technical teams assess that delivered combination. A photograph of a trolley with several loose components is not evidence of a working matched set. For an installed Excel+ system, compare an accepted repair or required handpiece replacement with a complete platform change. Preserve that option only where the applicable instructions, continuing supplies and qualified support remain available. Its age alone does not settle the decision."
      ],
      [
        "Read the catalog as a package, not a console specification",
        "Integra’s November 2017 Canadian Clarity brochure makes the separation clear: C7000 is the console with footswitch, C7001 the cart, and C7036 the 36 kHz handpiece with cleaning brush. It lists tips, tubing, a torque base and a sterilization tray separately. These historical codes illustrate omissions to catch; confirm the current local equivalents before ordering. The same table distinguishes C7412S, a box of five 36 kHz standard short tips, from C7412SEA, one tip. A price comparison that misses the suffix can be wrong before any service cost is added. Compare the exact approved tip, quantity, sterile status and remaining shelf life, not two lines both shortened to “CUSA tip”. Build the case basket around the actual intended work. Include every required disposable and the reusable items that must be ready. Do not assume that the number of tips in stock equals the number of fully supplied cases when another required component is missing."
      ],
      [
        "Frequency and application options are not interchangeable",
        "Integra’s US Clarity page describes an Expanded CEM nosecone for 23 kHz soft-tissue tips. That is a particular configuration; it does not make every 36 kHz handpiece, bone tip or external electrosurgical generator part of the same approved combination. Establish the exact compatibility and intended use from current applicable documentation. Keep optional laparoscopic or electrosurgical additions separate from the ordinary package unless the service requires them. Pay for a documented application the team will use, not a broad promise that the system does everything. The US page also explicitly excludes uterine-fibroid fragmentation, emulsification and aspiration; a general gynecological label must not obscure that limitation. Manufacturer comparisons of tissue fragmentation speed do not establish better clinical outcomes, shorter whole-case time or a lower repair bill. Use them as questions for an appropriate clinical evaluation, not as a purchasing ranking."
      ],
      [
        "A spare tip cannot replace an unavailable handpiece",
        "Consider two planned cases that require the same handpiece. Establish whether the first complete set can finish its approved processing route in time. If it cannot, another accepted prepared set may address the bottleneck more directly than a newer console. Extra disposable tips alone do not create a second ready handpiece. Include sterile-processing capacity, the prescribed trays and accessories, inspection and qualified maintenance in the budget. Have the technical team assess the system’s required aspiration, irrigation and cooling arrangements for the offered model. Do not improvise cleaning or operating instructions from a sales brochure. Service coverage should identify the console, handpieces and accessories separately and state what temporary replacement equipment actually contains. Compare a complete repair/backup arrangement with buying another incomplete unit. The better purchase is the sustainable surgical capability, with supplies for subsequent cases and an accepted return-to-service process."
      ]
    ],
