    "title": "Temperature management: compare the whole water-blanket system",
    "summary": "Blanketrol III and Medi-Therm III illustrate why the console price is only the start. Compare control functions, patient interfaces, water maintenance and support for the exact unit.",
    "reviewedAt": "2026-10-18",
    "sections": [
      [
        "Buy the required control function",
        "A water-circulating temperature-management unit belongs to a clinical service with defined indications, monitoring and staff responsibilities. Start with those requirements. A machine capable of heating and cooling water does not establish that every control mode or blanket is suitable for every patient or treatment. This comparison concerns external water-blanket systems. It does not select treatment temperatures or protocols, and it does not establish suitability for cardiopulmonary bypass. Have the clinical lead identify the required modes and patient interfaces; let biomedical engineering assess the exact configuration and acceptance requirements. That creates a useful specification before prices arrive."
      ],
      [
        "The model name needs a configuration beside it",
        "Gentherm’s 2026 Blanketrol III Model 233 manual distinguishes manual water control, automatic patient control, two gradient modes and monitoring alone. The system requires approved Gentherm accessories; automatic modes need a compatible 400 Series thermistor probe. Its side-panel diagram shows three water-circuit connections but one patient-probe input. Multiple connectors do not mean three independently controlled patients. The Stryker-hosted Medi-Therm III MTA6900 manual describes manual, automatic and monitor modes, with selectable cooling rates in automatic operation. Its accessory list specifies DBK9 connecting hoses and distinguishes reusable from single-patient blankets. These are documented configuration differences, not evidence that either machine produces better clinical outcomes or fails less often."
      ],
      [
        "Compare a bedside package with a bedside package",
        "An acceptable offer identifies the console, electrical version, blanket or wrap sizes, hose quantities, temperature probes and any reusable probe adapter. Include the first usable stock of patient interfaces. A seller photograph showing a console and coiled hose does not establish that the supplied combination is complete or compatible. Reusable and single-patient interfaces move spending to different places. For reusable stock, budget cleaning, inspection, storage and replacement after damage. For single-patient stock, budget replenishment and a realistic reserve. Evaluate the clinical team’s approved sizes and access needs before standardising: a cheaper interface that does not fit the planned work is not a substitute."
      ],
      [
        "Water maintenance can change the site decision",
        "Do not copy one machine’s water and cleaning routine to another. The cited Blanketrol revision specifies sterile or suitably filtered water and excludes distilled water; the cited MTA6900 manual specifies distilled water. Obtain the applicable instructions for the offered unit and build the required supply and maintenance process around them. These documents are not interchangeable maintenance recipes. Allocate responsibility, space and downtime for circuit maintenance as well as exterior cleaning. Assess the actual room, ventilation clearances, power supply, hose routing and storage arrangement. An inexpensive unit can be a poor fit if the hospital cannot sustain its required consumables or technical work. A new purchase does not remove those obligations."
      ],
      [
        "Test the economics against your own workload",
        "Consider a hypothetical package that costs 8,000 more upfront but reduces accepted patient-interface spending by 40 per completed episode. At 200 episodes a year over three years, that difference totals 24,000. After the extra capital, the remaining advantage is 16,000 before differences in cleaning, service, stock losses and downtime. These are planning inputs, not quoted prices or an assumed saving for either named model. Repeat the calculation at the likely lower workload. At 50 episodes a year, the same three-year interface difference is only 6,000, leaving the higher-capital option 2,000 behind before other costs. A recurring-cost advantage matters only across activity that the service is realistically equipped and staffed to deliver."
      ],
      [
        "A legacy manual is not a service commitment",
        "The MTA6900 document is revision C dated January 2011. Its availability on an OEM technical site helps identify the system; it does not confirm current parts, warranty or support for a proposed used unit. Require a named service route, the applicable maintenance record and a documented acceptance test before relying on that unit. Retaining an accepted system can be sensible when supplies and service are sustainable. Replace it when a required clinical function, support gap or operating constraint justifies the change. Neither heater wattage nor a seller’s claim of durability establishes patient-level performance or comparative reliability."
      ]
    ],
