      "title": "Patient warming: choose the blanket before the box",
      "dek": "Forced-air and resistive warming put different demands on supplies, cleaning and surgical access. Compare a usable patient interface and its full workflow before choosing the controller.",
      "researchedAt": "2026-10-18",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "Start at the patient, not the equipment cupboard",
          "paragraphs": [
            "The purchase decision begins with the body area available for warming, patient size, surgical position and access the team must preserve. A controller with several settings can still be the wrong purchase if its supplied blanket obstructs the planned procedure. Have the clinical team define acceptable interfaces for the actual case mix before standardising a room.",
            "Map where warming is required through the patient journey. A device dedicated to the operating room may not solve shortages in preparation or recovery. Count simultaneous demand and transfer arrangements separately from the number of cases per day. Clinical indications, monitoring and temperature settings belong to the approved protocol and applicable device instructions."
          ],
          "sourceIds": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Forced air: distinguish the controller from the disposable",
          "paragraphs": [
            "Solventum’s catalogue describes the Bair Hugger 775 as compatible with its warming blankets and gowns, while the patient-adjustable 875 is for preoperative and postoperative settings and warming gowns. The two units therefore should not be treated as interchangeable just because they share a brand.",
            "The 62200 upper-body blanket is a single-use interface with two hose ports for positioning. Other shapes serve other access needs. Specify the approved blanket stock, compatible unit, hose and mounting arrangement together. A low console price can leave a recurring supply obligation larger than the equipment saving."
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "bair-catalogue",
            "bair-62200"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Resistive pads: reusable does not mean always available",
          "paragraphs": [
            "Gentherm’s Astopad DUO310 uses reusable resistive blankets or pads and provides two independently controlled connections. Its COV range includes the COV105 for lithotomy and COV155 for upper-body coverage; SOF and ROE options integrate warming into table-pad arrangements, with availability varying by country. A battery configuration also needs to be identified in the offer.",
            "Treat the patient-contact pad as an asset with its own inventory and replacement budget. Demonstrate how the proposed size works with the actual table, positioning accessories and surgical access. A second socket is useful only when the approved configuration and clinical workflow can use it. It is not a reason to assume one controller can meet every simultaneous warming demand."
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "astopad"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Price the cleaning cycle as carefully as the supply carton",
          "paragraphs": [
            "For disposable interfaces, calculate cost per completed case using the actual mix of shapes and sizes, including unopened stock that expires and stock lost through damaged packaging. Establish who replenishes each care area. Avoid relying on a cheap alternative solely because its connector appears to fit; compatibility must be documented for the exact system.",
            "For reusable pads, include the initial inventory, compatible cleaning products, staff time, inspection, storage and replacement after damage. Stock must cover pads in use and those unavailable during cleaning or repair. If two rooms operate simultaneously, buying only two pads leaves no spare during turnaround. The correct reserve depends on the observed workflow and accepted reprocessing instructions."
          ],
          "sourceIds": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "A cost example that does not assume a pad lasts forever",
          "paragraphs": [
            "Suppose a disposable interface costs 18 per case. A hypothetical reusable pad costs 600, achieves 120 accepted uses before replacement and needs 8 of cleaning and handling per use. Its allocated interface cost is 600 ÷ 120 + 8 = 13 per case. At 800 comparable cases, that is 10,400 versus 14,400 for disposables, before controller, inventory, service and other differences.",
            "If the pad achieves only 50 accepted uses, its cost becomes 600 ÷ 50 + 8 = 20 per case, exceeding the disposable assumption. These figures are illustrative, not product prices or predicted service lives. Use an evidence-based replacement allowance and test a shorter-life scenario before presenting savings to a budget committee."
          ],
          "sourceIds": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Choose on demonstrated fit",
          "paragraphs": [
            "Observe setup, patient access, cable or hose routing, cleaning and return to service with the proposed package. Include the people responsible for anesthesia, nursing, infection prevention and equipment maintenance. Record whether additional stock or a different interface fixes the problem before replacing a suitable controller.",
            "Avoid declaring either technology safer from a seller’s infection claim, or more economical from the absence of a disposable blanket alone. The defensible choice is the accepted system that delivers the required warming workflow with sustainable staffing, supplies and support. Retain functioning equipment where it still meets that standard."
          ],
          "sourceIds": []
        }
      ],
      "verdict": "Choose an approved warming system around the required patient coverage, surgical access and care location. Forced-air disposable interfaces require dependable replenishment; reusable resistive pads require enough stock and cleaning capacity. Neither approach earns a universal infection, reliability or cost advantage from its product brochure.",
