      "dek": "Blankets, irrigation solutions and IV fluids do not create the same storage requirement. Compare compartment controls, actual load fit, stock rotation and installation before buying a larger cabinet.",
      "researchedAt": "2026-10-18",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "Define the load before choosing the cabinet",
          "paragraphs": [
            "A warming cabinet stores selected materials under controlled conditions. It is not a general-purpose approval to warm whatever fits inside. Start with separate lists for blankets, the exact irrigation products and any IV fluids. Record the container, permitted temperature and time, stock rotation and intended use after removal.",
            "A cabinet’s advertised maximum temperature is a capability limit, not the setting for every load. Its application statement also does not establish permission to warm blood, medicines or other unlisted products. If the requirement is blood or fluid warming during administration, commission that specific device decision separately."
          ],
          "sourceIds": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Single and dual compartments solve different problems",
          "paragraphs": [
            "STERIS lists AMSCO single-compartment capacities of 3.1 or 4.2 cubic feet, according to depth. The dual cabinet has two independently controlled chambers; the 24-inch-depth version divides its 15.8 cubic feet into 4.2 above and 11.6 below. That split matters more than the headline total when most demand belongs to one load type.",
            "The older AMSCO SD916 technical sheet distinguishes IV and irrigation/blanket controls in the single or upper chamber from the lower chamber’s control arrangement. Do not assume every chamber of a used dual cabinet has the same permitted purpose. Match the actual generation and compartment to its applicable instructions before allocating stock."
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "single",
            "dual",
            "tech"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "The solution label is part of the specification",
          "paragraphs": [
            "B. Braun’s September 2025 irrigation FAQ gives different warming time limits for TITAN XL and PIC containers at the same stated temperature. It also distinguishes conditions with or without an overwrap. Those product-specific differences show why a single generic “fluid warmer” rule is inadequate; use the current instructions for the actual solution and container.",
            "Have the responsible clinical and pharmacy teams define the permitted inventory and removal process. Labels or records should make warmed stock identifiable and allow the team to act when its approved time expires. Filling every shelf is not efficient if low-use products sit warm until they must be discarded."
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "fluids"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Size ready stock around replenishment",
          "paragraphs": [
            "Suppose a service needs 12 warmed blankets during a two-hour peak. A hypothetical cabinet that holds 16 in its approved loading arrangement may cover that demand if replenishment and warming time are dependable. A larger cabinet is justified when it solves a demonstrated shortage, not simply because the daily total exceeds 16.",
            "For solutions, model stock in units of the actual containers rather than litres of cabinet volume. Shelves, packaging and required airflow constrain useful capacity. Test the proposed arrangement with a representative load and the applicable acceptance process; an empty cabinet reaching its setpoint does not demonstrate the performance of a fully loaded one."
          ],
          "sourceIds": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Location can be worth more than extra capacity",
          "paragraphs": [
            "Measure door swing, working space, transport route, electrical service and access for maintenance. Recessing a cabinet can save visible floor space but adds building and service-access work. A convenient cabinet may reduce retrieval time; quantify that workflow with staff rather than assuming every relocation creates usable labour savings.",
            "STERIS offers glass or solid doors and optional electronic recording on these models. Visual inventory and recorded temperatures serve different tasks. If recording is part of the purchase, include retrieval software or access, retention and an owner who reviews exceptions. A USB socket alone does not establish a functioning monitoring process."
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "single",
            "dual"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Compare waste and installation over one period",
          "paragraphs": [
            "In a hypothetical three-year comparison, Cabinet A costs US$5,000 installed and Cabinet B costs US$8,000. If B avoids US$100 worth of discarded stock each month under an otherwise equivalent accepted workflow, the avoided cost is US$3,600 over 36 months. That exceeds its US$3,000 extra installation cost by US$600 before energy, maintenance and other differences. At only US$50 worth of avoided waste a month, the avoided cost is US$1,800 and does not recover the extra capital.",
            "Use observed demand and disposal records to support those inputs. Sometimes the better investment is more frequent replenishment, clearer rotation or repair of an existing cabinet. Buy a larger or divided model when its usable capacity and controls address the actual constraint."
          ],
          "sourceIds": []
        }
      ],
      "verdict": "Buy capacity for the permitted contents and their turnover. Choose separate controlled compartments when the accepted loads require different conditions; choose a smaller cabinet when replenishment can meet demand without excess warmed stock. Confirm both cabinet and fluid instructions before treating any compartment as suitable.",
