      "title": "Surgical slush machines: price the drapes before the deal",
      "dek": "A slush machine depends on a compatible sterile interface and a sustainable supply agreement. Compare exact generations, included warming functions and cost per usable case before buying or accepting a loaned unit.",
      "researchedAt": "2026-10-18",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "Start with a defined surgical service",
          "paragraphs": [
            "A surgical slush purchase should follow a clinical decision that the service needs this method of preparing a surgical solution. The procurement task is to make that approved workflow dependable: the correct machine, sterile interface, solution, staff training and support must all be available together.",
            "A cooling basin is not evidence of sterilization capability. Do not infer that the machine makes an unsuitable solution or compromised drape safe. Clinical use, solution selection and preparation belong to the applicable instructions and hospital protocol. The buying comparison should establish readiness and cost without turning into a preparation recipe."
          ],
          "sourceIds": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "Keep the generations straight",
          "paragraphs": [
            "Ecolab’s EU specification identifies an ORS-1075EU slush machine, ORS-K24EU Auto-Stir and ORS-2038EU solution warmer. Its table names the compatible ORS-130, ORS-320, ORS-321 and ORS-325 disc-drapes and describes them as sterile and single-use. Those identifiers are more useful than an offer headed simply “Hush Slush”.",
            "Medline Microtek’s current product page describes Hush Slush 2.0 with an integrated fluid warmer and a twist-lock drape arrangement. Do not transfer the older EU system’s drape, electrical or accessory specifications to a differently labelled generation. Require the exact model, revision, applicable instructions and supplied components to agree."
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "legacy-spec",
            "microtek"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Choose the package for the work",
          "paragraphs": [
            "If the approved requirement is slush alone, establish whether a warming function adds useful capacity or duplicates equipment already serving the room. If both functions are needed, compare the actual ready-to-use combination, including the correct covering arrangement, control access, installation and maintenance. Two advertised functions are not two usable functions when a required component is missing.",
            "Demonstrate the package with the people who prepare it and maintain the sterile field. Include setup, movement around the room, return to service and storage. Ask for evidence of the performance required by the local workflow; do not translate a manufacturer’s claims about soft slush or reduced nursing work into proven clinical superiority or a guaranteed staffing saving."
          ],
          "sourceIds": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "The recurring order can decide the economics",
          "paragraphs": [
            "Identify the exact drape code, pack quantity, usable shelf life on delivery and supply route. Include expected opening losses and the stock reserved for unplanned demand. Availability of a physically similar drape is not proof of compatibility. A system that depends on one particular consumable deserves a credible response to a stock interruption.",
            "For a low-volume service, minimum order quantities can matter more than unit price. Suppose a hypothetical carton contains 24 approved drapes at 80 each, but only 12 are used before the remaining stock expires. The carton costs 1,920 and its effective drape cost is 160 per completed case. If all 24 are used, it is 80. This is an inventory example, not a market price or expiry forecast."
          ],
          "sourceIds": []
        },
        {
          "heading": "A placement offer still has a price",
          "paragraphs": [
            "Medline describes loaned, rented and sold units. Compare the written terms: minimum consumable purchases, eligible product codes, annual increases, servicing, replacement cover and what happens if activity falls or the agreement ends. A supplied machine may be valuable, but the funding comes from the complete agreement.",
            "For illustration, a commitment to buy 150 drapes a year when the service uses 100 creates 50 units of excess annual purchasing unless stock can legitimately carry forward or serve other accepted demand. Calculate that obligation before comparing it with a capital purchase. Avoid crediting a placement scheme with savings that depend on cases the hospital does not expect to perform."
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "microtek"
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Traceability includes the disposable",
          "paragraphs": [
            "FDA recall Z-1141-2021 concerned ORS-320 lot D192619A because slits could compromise the sterile barrier. The recall was initiated in January 2021 and terminated in January 2023. It is a specific historical lot issue, not evidence that every current ORS-320 is defective or that a proposed package is cleared.",
            "Record the received drape codes and lots alongside machine identity, expiry, acceptance and service documents. Keep a clinically approved contingency for an unavailable machine or supply. Retain an accepted system when it can sustain the service; replace it when a documented capability, supply or support problem makes the complete alternative worthwhile."
          ],
          "sourceIds": [
            "fda-lot"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verdict": "Buy or accept a slush system only for a defined, clinically approved service with compatible drapes, maintenance and contingency arrangements. Compare purchase and placement offers over realistic activity. A low machine price cannot compensate for an unsupported interface or an uneconomic supply commitment.",
