    "sections": [
      [
        "Start with the function the hospital needs",
        "Counting support records items entering and leaving a case. Detection searches for compatible tagged items in a defined area. A combined system may do both, but its supported items and detection locations still need checking. Do not compare these as interchangeable features simply because both proposals use the phrase “retained-item prevention.” For a service whose main difficulty is reconciling and documenting sponge counts, counting support may address the identified task. If the proposal must also help locate a missing sponge, require that additional labelled function. Keep the hospital’s complete counting and discrepancy-response policy in place, including the items the technology does not cover."
      ],
      [
        "Compare named packages without extending their claims",
        "Medtronic lists Situate Detection System X package 01-0047 with console, body scanner and room scanner; 01-0043 is the console alone. It uses RF detection with Situate tagged cotton and supplements manual counting. A low price for a console is therefore not a price for the complete detection package or its ongoing supplies. Stryker describes SurgiCount+ as counting, tracking and locating surgical sponges within the operating room and sterile field. Its 2026 supply catalogue identifies RFID-tagged gauze, lap sponges, OR towels and vaginal sponges. Compare the exact offered generation and its instructions; a brand name alone does not establish identical capabilities across older and newer systems."
      ],
      [
        "Make the coverage boundary visible",
        "Map the hospital’s actual sponge and other counted-item list against the proposed catalogue. Record uncovered items explicitly. An instrument, needle or untagged sponge does not gain electronic coverage merely because the room has a scanner. Procurement substitutions need clinical review of the item and its compatibility, rather than a visual match to an existing product. The Situate in-service guide describes limits related to positioning, nearby metal and electrical interference, along with specific precautions for implanted or external electronic devices. These belong in clinical engineering and theatre acceptance. An incomplete or faulted scan is not a completed negative result. Staff training and the exact current instructions remain necessary; a purchasing guide cannot supply the scanning procedure."
      ],
      [
        "Buy a usable record, not just a dashboard",
        "Situate stores completed scan records by case with date and time. Stryker advertises a reporting dashboard and EMR integration for SurgiCount+. These are different documentation propositions. Test the offered system’s route from a completed case to the hospital record, including patient association, access, retention and a practical response to network or device outages. Budget interface implementation, software access and support where applicable. Have IT establish which functions remain usable during an outage before approval. SurgiCount+ is also presented with Triton blood-loss functions; evaluate those separately if needed. Their presence is not evidence that the sponge-counting task is performed better."
      ],
      [
        "The supply premium follows every covered case",
        "Use the difference between compatible supplies and the accepted alternative, not the total price of cotton alone. Include pack sizes, opened-but-unused items, wastage, minimum commitments and the clinical suitability of the available range. A nominally free reader can still create a substantial recurring obligation. For a hypothetical annual comparison, 1,500 cases using 20 items each means 30,000 items. An assumed US$0.65 premium adds US$19,500; US$5,000 in annual software and service brings the recurring increment to US$24,500, or US$16.33 per case. At 3,000 otherwise equivalent cases it becomes US$44,000, or US$14.67 each. Initial equipment, implementation and training would be additional. These are planning assumptions, not quotes or proof of cost effectiveness."
      ],
      [
        "Approve a supported workflow, then monitor it",
        "Select the package that addresses the defined task with a reliable supply route, appropriate training and an approved response when the system is unavailable. During implementation, review coverage gaps, completed documentation, exceptions and staff work. Do not promise a particular reduction in retained items or avoided claims costs from a manufacturer’s promotional statistic. Stryker’s brochure expressly treats its zero-retained-sponge message as an aspiration, not a guarantee. Comparative clinical outcomes require separate evidence and qualified review. A sound buying decision can still distinguish functions, missing components and recurring cost without inventing a clinical winner."
      ]
    ],
    "socialTitle": "Surgical sponge systems: counting and detection are different purchases",
